{%- comment -%}
  fye-buybox-finish — the surface-finish chooser for plain wedding rings.

  Built 18/09/2026. Rendered by main-product inside the `is_plain` branch,
  directly ABOVE engraving, because both are personalisation and they come
  after metal, profile, width, weight and size. Ed, 18/09/2026.

  ── WHY A TOGGLE AND NOT A ROW ────────────────────────────────────────────

  Every ring is polished. Most shoppers will leave it there, so the control
  has to stay quiet until it is wanted. It is therefore the SAME Yes/No
  segmented pattern as engraving (`.pdp__seg`), not a permanently open row of
  48 choices, and Yes OPENS THE PICKER straight away — the toggle would
  otherwise be a switch with nothing behind it.

  Cancelling out of the picker LEAVES THE TOGGLE ON, showing Polished with a
  Change button. Ed, 18/09/2026: snapping back to No throws away the fact
  that the shopper asked the question, and they then have to find the control
  again to ask it a second time.

  ── THE FINISH LIST ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  48 choices: Polished, plus Hockley Mint's 47. The HM NUMBER IS THE ORDER
  REFERENCE and must travel with the choice, so the property reads

      Finish: Matt with Bevelled Edges (HM 38)

  Customer-facing names are tidied from HM's; the number is shown quietly
  under the name so a shopper who rings up can quote it, and so the workshop
  and the page cannot drift apart.

  DELIBERATELY NOT GROUPED. Ed, 18/09/2026. A first pass had eight group tabs
  (Matt and brushed, Frosted, Milled…). They are HM's manufacturing families,
  not how anyone shops: a shopper who wants "a line down the middle" does not
  know whether that is Grooves or Milled, and the tabs hid forty of the
  forty-eight behind a guess. One flat grid with a search field instead —
  Polished first, then HM's own order, which runs plain to decorated.

  THE HAND ENGRAVED SEVEN have no names of their own in HM's list, so they are
  named "Hand Engraved Pattern 1–7" here and the modal says in words that they
  are cut into the OUTSIDE of the band. They are real engraving and they are
  not the engraving option below, which is a message inside the ring; a
  shopper who confuses the two orders the wrong thing.

  ── THE PHOTOGRAPHY IS THE CONSTRAINT ─────────────────────────────────────

  HM's finish photographs are 200x200 JPGs on a white ground, one ring at a
  slight angle. They hold up to about 120px and go soft above it, which is
  why the tiles are ~112px and why the gallery is NOT switched to the chosen
  finish: the hero is a 1254px product photograph and swapping a 200px
  thumbnail into it upscales by five. The choice is echoed as an 88px
  thumbnail in the buy box instead, where the source is sharp.

  They are uploaded to Shopify Files as `rf-finish-<slug>.jpg`, with `-yellow`
  and `-rose` variants, so `file_url` resolves them with no metaobject and no
  app. COLOUR FOLLOWS THE RING: yellow gold takes `-yellow`, rose gold takes
  `-rose`, and white gold, platinum and palladium all share the white set,
  which is how HM shoots them.

  HM shoot no polished ring, so the three polished photographs are ours:
  `gold_polished.png`, `rose_polished.png` and `platinum_polished.png`, one
  per metal family, following the same colour rule. Ed, 18/09/2026. They are
  1254px, the product-photography size rather than HM's 200, so every use
  here asks the CDN for the width it actually needs. `file_url` alone would
  ship a 1254px PNG into a 112px tile.

  HM shoot a `_thin` variant for bands of 4mm and under. Those are NOT in
  Files yet, so width does not change the photograph today. When they are
  uploaded, append the suffix here from `filters.band_width`; nothing else
  needs to change.

  ── THE UPLIFT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Ed, 18/09/2026: polished is included, every Hockley Mint finish is £50, and
  the seven hand engraved patterns are £100, because those are cut by hand.

  Charged the way engraving is, and for the same reason: a fee variant on a
  hidden product, posted as its own cart line, so the price the buy box shows
  is the price the customer is actually taken through checkout at. The two
  variants live on `Custom Finish`; their IDs and the two figures are section
  settings, so changing a price is a customiser job and not a code one.

  Each tile carries its own fee and fee variant. fye-finish.js copies the
  applied tile's pair onto this block's root, where fye-ui.js reads them
  exactly as it reads engraving's, which is why the total and the cart line
  cannot disagree.

  ── WHAT THE ORDER CARRIES ────────────────────────────────────────────────

      properties[Finish]   "Polished" | "Matt with Bevelled Edges (HM 38)"

  Polished is sent EXPLICITLY, on every plain ring, whether or not the shopper
  opened the picker. An absent property would mean "nobody asked", which is
  not the same thing as "polished" and is not what the workshop needs to read.

  The field is a real named input inside the buy-box form, so the wishlist —
  which saves what the buy box currently holds, via context: 'form' — picks
  the finish up with no change to fye-wishlist-button.

  ── layout: 'pdx' (27/09/2026, the CRO redesign) ──────────────────────

  Passed only by fye-pdx-ring, behind the preview gate. The No/Yes toggle is
  replaced by the handoff's four cards: Polished, Matt and Vertical Brushed
  as quick picks (a tap applies them, no modal), and a fourth "View more
  finishes" card that opens the same picker. A finish chosen in the picker
  that is not one of the three shows on that fourth card.

  The picker gains CATEGORY CHIPS (All, Polished, Matt & brushed, Part matt,
  Edge details, Lines & patterns) beside the search, and a ring total in its
  footer. This reverses the 18/09 "deliberately not grouped" call for this
  layout only, because Ed's redesign asks for it; search still covers every
  finish and "All" is the default. The handoff named five categories and
  left half the list in none of them, so a sixth, "Lines & patterns", holds
  the grooves, milled, frosted motif and hand engraved finishes. `data-cat`
  on each tile is the only change the old layout sees, and it reads nothing
  from it.

  Parameters: product, settings (the section's), layout (optional)
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign p = product
  assign s = settings
  assign cf = p.metafields.custom

  comment
    Colour suffix. White gold, platinum and palladium share HM's white set.
  endcomment
  assign metal_now = cf.ring_metal | append: ''
  assign tone = ''
  if metal_now contains 'Yellow'
    assign tone = '-yellow'
  elsif metal_now contains 'Rose'
    assign tone = '-rose'
  endif

  comment
    The polished photographs, same colour rule. These three are 1254px, so
    each use asks the CDN for the width it needs. `&width=` is exactly what
    image_url emits; file_url is the only way to reach a file by name.
  endcomment
  assign polished_file = 'platinum_polished.png'
  if metal_now contains 'Yellow'
    assign polished_file = 'gold_polished.png'
  elsif metal_now contains 'Rose'
    assign polished_file = 'rose_polished.png'
  endif
  assign polished_url = polished_file | file_url
  assign polished_tile = polished_url | append: '&width=224'
  assign polished_thumb = polished_url | append: '&width=176'
  assign polished_small = polished_url | append: '&width=88'

  comment
    The uplift. Settings rather than constants so a price change is a
    customiser job; the defaults are the two Custom Finish variants.
  endcomment
  assign fee_plain = s.finish_price_pennies | default: 5000
  assign fee_hand = s.finish_engraved_price_pennies | default: 10000
  assign var_plain = s.finish_variant_id | default: '59922131681664'
  assign var_hand = s.finish_engraved_variant_id | default: '59922131714432'
  assign label_plain = fee_plain | money_without_trailing_zeros
  assign label_hand = fee_hand | money_without_trailing_zeros

  assign pdx = false
  if layout == 'pdx'
    assign pdx = true
  endif

  comment
    Category per HM number, for the pdx picker's chips. Anything not listed
    is a line or pattern. Polished has its own chip and no HM number.
  endcomment
  assign cat_matt = '~24~67~69~68~86~65~66~'
  assign cat_part = '~19~35~30~'
  assign cat_edge = '~6~2~38~39~25~5~47~94~22~23~3~4~46~84~85~'
-%}

{%- capture finish_rows -%}
  24|Matt|matt;
  67|Vertical Brushed|vertical-brushed;
  69|Diagonal Brushed|diagonal-brushed;
  68|File Matt|file-matt;
  19|Half Matt, Half Polished|half-matt-half-polished;
  35|Three-Quarter Matt|three-quarter-matt;
  30|Matt in Three Bands|matt-in-three-bands;
  6|Matt Centre, Polished Edges|matt-centre-polished-edges;
  2|Matt Centre, Stepped Edges|matt-centre-stepped-edges;
  38|Matt with Bevelled Edges|matt-with-bevelled-edges;
  39|Matt Centre with Boxed Bevelled Edges|matt-centre-with-boxed-bevelled-edges;
  25|Matt with Flat Groove and Bevelled Edges|matt-with-flat-groove-and-bevelled-edges;
  5|Matt with Inset Milled Edges|matt-with-inset-milled-edges;
  47|Matt with Inset Milled Edge and Polished Step|matt-with-inset-milled-edge-and-polished-step;
  94|Matt with Feathered Edges|matt-with-feathered-edges;
  17|Matt with Centre Groove|matt-with-centre-groove;
  34|Matt with Centre V-Groove|matt-with-centre-v-groove;
  27|Matt with Twin Centre Grooves|matt-with-twin-centre-grooves;
  18|Matt with Offset Double Line|matt-with-offset-double-line;
  37|Matt with Double Offset Lines|matt-with-double-offset-lines;
  21|Centre V-Groove|centre-v-groove;
  22|Bevelled Edges|bevelled-edges;
  23|Lined Edges|lined-edges;
  95|Centre Chevrons|centre-chevrons;
  1|Milled Centre|milled-centre;
  3|Milled Edges|milled-edges;
  4|Polished with Inset Milled Edges|polished-with-inset-milled-edges;
  46|Polished with Double Milled Edges|polished-with-double-milled-edges;
  86|Frosted|frosted;
  84|Frosted with Stepped Edges|frosted-with-stepped-edges;
  85|Frosted with Chamfered Edges|frosted-with-chamfered-edges;
  87|Frosted with Polished Tramlines|frosted-with-polished-tramlines;
  88|Frosted with Flat Centre Line|frosted-with-flat-centre-line;
  89|Frosted with Stars and Stepped Edges|frosted-with-stars-and-stepped-edges;
  90|Frosted with Concave Wave|frosted-with-concave-wave;
  91|Frosted with Leaves|frosted-with-leaves;
  92|Frosted with Oval Spots|frosted-with-oval-spots;
  93|Matt with Leaves|matt-with-leaves;
  65|Bark|bark;
  66|Diamond Wheel|diamond-wheel;
  70|Hand Engraved Pattern 1|hand-engraved-1;
  71|Hand Engraved Pattern 2|hand-engraved-2;
  72|Hand Engraved Pattern 3|hand-engraved-3;
  73|Hand Engraved Pattern 4|hand-engraved-4;
  74|Hand Engraved Pattern 5|hand-engraved-5;
  75|Hand Engraved Pattern 6|hand-engraved-6;
  76|Hand Engraved Pattern 7|hand-engraved-7
{%- endcapture -%}
{%- assign finishes = finish_rows | split: ';' -%}

{%- comment -%}
  data-fee-price and data-fee-variant start empty because Polished is free.
  fye-finish.js rewrites both as the applied finish changes, and fye-ui.js
  reads them here the same way it reads engraving's.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div class="pdp__finish{% if pdx %} pdx__finish{% endif %}" data-fye-finish data-fee-price="0" data-fee-variant="">

  {%- if pdx -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The three quick picks are the Polished tile plus HM 24 and HM 67, named
      by their property string so fye-finish.js finds the matching tile in
      the picker and applies it through the same code path.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign more_count = finishes.size | minus: 2 -%}
    <p class="pdx__label">{{ s.finish_label | default: 'Finish' }} <span class="pdx__labelval"><span data-fye-finish-name>Polished</span> · <span data-pdx-finish-price>Standard</span></span></p>
    <div class="pdx__fquick" role="radiogroup" aria-label="{{ s.finish_label | default: 'Finish' | escape }}">
      <button type="button" class="pdx__fcard is-on" role="radio" aria-checked="true" data-fye-finish-quick="Polished">
        <span class="pdx__fcardimg"><img src="{{ polished_tile }}" alt="" width="112" height="112" loading="lazy"></span>
        <span class="pdx__fcardname">Polished</span>
        <span class="pdx__fcardprice">Standard</span>
      </button>
      {%- assign quick = 'Matt|24|matt,Vertical Brushed|67|vertical-brushed' | split: ',' -%}
      {%- for qrow in quick -%}
        {%- assign qb = qrow | split: '|' -%}
        {%- assign q_img = 'rf-finish-' | append: qb[2] | append: tone | append: '.jpg' -%}
        <button type="button" class="pdx__fcard" role="radio" aria-checked="false" data-fye-finish-quick="{{ qb[0] }} (HM {{ qb[1] }})">
          <span class="pdx__fcardimg"><img src="{{ q_img | file_url }}" alt="" width="112" height="112" loading="lazy"></span>
          <span class="pdx__fcardname">{{ qb[0] }}</span>
          <span class="pdx__fcardprice">+{{ label_plain }}</span>
        </button>
      {%- endfor -%}
      <button type="button" class="pdx__fcard pdx__fcard--more" data-fye-finish-open data-fye-finish-more>
        <span class="pdx__fcardmore" data-fye-finish-more-name>View more finishes</span>
        <span class="pdx__fcardprice" data-fye-finish-more-meta data-default="{{ more_count }} additional options">{{ more_count }} additional options</span>
      </button>
    </div>
    <p class="pdx__help">{{ s.pdx_finish_help | default: 'Every ring is polished as standard. Finishes are cut into the outside of the band, which is separate from engraving a message inside.' }}</p>
  {%- else -%}
  <div class="pdp__finishhead">
    <p class="pdp__label">
      {{ s.finish_label | default: 'Custom finish' }}
      <span class="pdp__labelval" data-fye-finish-hint>{{ s.finish_hint | default: 'Polished as standard' }}</span>
    </p>

    <div class="pdp__seg" role="radiogroup" aria-label="{{ s.finish_label | default: 'Custom finish' | escape }}">
      <button type="button" role="radio" class="is-on" aria-checked="true" data-fye-finish-set="no">No</button>
      <button type="button" role="radio" aria-checked="false" data-fye-finish-set="yes">Yes</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    The chosen finish. Hidden until the toggle is on; it then shows Polished,
    so the shopper can see that "yes" has not silently changed anything yet.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="pdp__finishpick" data-fye-finish-pick hidden>
    <img class="pdp__finishthumb"
         src="{{ polished_thumb }}"
         alt=""
         width="88"
         height="88"
         loading="lazy"
         data-fye-finish-thumb>
    <span class="pdp__finishtxt">
      <span class="pdp__finishname" data-fye-finish-name>Polished</span>
      <span class="pdp__finishmeta" data-fye-finish-meta>Standard finish</span>
    </span>
    <span class="pdp__finishacts">
      <button type="button" class="pdp__finishreset" data-fye-finish-reset hidden>Back to polished</button>
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--outline pdp__finishopen" data-fye-finish-open>
        {{ s.finish_change_label | default: 'Change' }}
      </button>
    </span>
  </div>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Always enabled, always sent — see the header. Its value is rewritten by
    fye-finish.js as the choice changes.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <input type="hidden" name="properties[Finish]" value="Polished" data-fye-finish-prop>

  {%- comment -%}
    ---- The picker modal ----
    Same shell as the diamond picker (`.pdp__modal` / scrim / panel), one set
    of markup at every width: the grid reflows and becomes a sheet on a phone.
    Never a second copy of the content.

    Tiles are rendered by Liquid rather than fetched, because the list is
    fixed, small and the same on every plain ring. No request, no loading
    state, and the grid is in the DOM for search engines and for anyone with
    JS off, who still sees the finishes even though they cannot select one.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="pdp__modal" data-fye-finish-modal hidden>
    <div class="pdp__modalscrim" data-fye-finish-close></div>

    <div class="pdp__modalpanel" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="pdp-finish-title" tabindex="-1" data-fye-finish-panel>
      <div class="pdp__modalhead">
        <div>
          <h2 class="pdp__modaltitle" id="pdp-finish-title">{{ s.finish_heading | default: 'Choose your finish' }}</h2>
          <p class="pdp__modalsub">
            {{ s.finish_note | default: 'Every ring is polished as standard. Hand engraved patterns are cut into the outside of the band by our finishers, which is different from a custom engraved message inside the ring.' }}
          </p>
        </div>

        <button type="button" class="pdp__modalx" data-fye-finish-close aria-label="Close">
          {%- render 'icon', name: 'close' -%}
        </button>
      </div>

      {%- if pdx -%}
        <div class="pdx__fcats" role="group" aria-label="Finish type">
          {%- assign cats = 'all|All,polished|Polished,matt|Matt & brushed,part|Part matt,edge|Edge details,lines|Lines & patterns' | split: ',' -%}
          {%- for c in cats -%}
            {%- assign cb = c | split: '|' -%}
            <button type="button" class="pdx__fcat{% if forloop.first %} is-on{% endif %}" aria-pressed="{% if forloop.first %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}" data-fye-finish-cat="{{ cb[0] }}">{{ cb[1] }}</button>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
      <div class="pdp__modaltools">
        <label class="visually-hidden" for="pdp-finish-search">Search finishes</label>
        <input class="pdp__input pdp__finishsearch"
               type="search"
               id="pdp-finish-search"
               placeholder="{{ s.finish_search_label | default: 'Search finishes' | escape }}"
               autocomplete="off"
               data-fye-finish-search>
        <p class="pdp__count" data-fye-finish-count aria-live="polite">48 finishes</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pdp__finishgrid" role="radiogroup" aria-label="{{ s.finish_heading | default: 'Choose your finish' | escape }}" data-fye-finish-grid>

        {%- comment -%}
          Polished first, and marked as the default in words. It carries no
          fee and no fee variant, so choosing it adds nothing to the total
          and posts no second cart line. No price is printed on it either: a
          "+£0" beside forty-seven prices invites the comparison rather than
          settling it.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <button type="button"
                class="pdp__ftile is-on"
                role="radio"
                aria-checked="true"
                data-fye-ftile
                data-name="Polished"
                data-prop="Polished"
                data-hm=""
                data-fee="0"
                data-fee-variant=""
                data-fee-label=""
                data-search="polished default standard"
                data-cat="polished">
          <span class="pdp__ftileimg">
            <img src="{{ polished_tile }}"
                 alt="A polished {{ metal_now | escape }} band"
                 width="112"
                 height="112"
                 loading="lazy">
          </span>
          <span class="pdp__ftilename">Polished</span>
          <span class="pdp__ftilehm">Standard finish</span>
          <span class="pdp__ftilemark" aria-hidden="true">{%- render 'icon', name: 'check' -%}</span>
        </button>

        {%- for row in finishes -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign bits = row | strip | split: '|'
            assign hm = bits[0] | strip
            assign name = bits[1] | strip
            assign slug = bits[2] | strip
          -%}
          {%- if slug == blank -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
          {%- assign asset_name = 'rf-finish-' | append: slug | append: tone | append: '.jpg' -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            The seven hand engraved patterns are cut by hand and cost £100;
            everything else is £50. The slug is the test because it is the
            same string the photograph is named after, so a new hand engraved
            pattern picks up the right price by being named like its fellows.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign hm_key = '~' | append: hm | append: '~'
            if cat_matt contains hm_key
              assign cat = 'matt'
            elsif cat_part contains hm_key
              assign cat = 'part'
            elsif cat_edge contains hm_key
              assign cat = 'edge'
            else
              assign cat = 'lines'
            endif
            assign fee = fee_plain
            assign fee_label = label_plain
            assign fee_variant = var_plain
            if slug contains 'hand-engraved'
              assign fee = fee_hand
              assign fee_label = label_hand
              assign fee_variant = var_hand
            endif
          -%}

          <button type="button"
                  class="pdp__ftile"
                  role="radio"
                  aria-checked="false"
                  data-fye-ftile
                  data-name="{{ name | escape }}"
                  data-prop="{{ name | append: ' (HM ' | append: hm | append: ')' | escape }}"
                  data-hm="HM {{ hm }}"
                  data-fee="{{ fee }}"
                  data-fee-variant="{{ fee_variant }}"
                  data-fee-label="+{{ fee_label }}"
                  data-search="{{ name | downcase | escape }} hm{{ hm }} {{ hm }}"
                  data-cat="{{ cat }}">
            <span class="pdp__ftileimg">
              <img src="{{ asset_name | file_url }}"
                   alt="{{ name | escape }} finish on a {{ metal_now | escape }} band"
                   width="112"
                   height="112"
                   loading="lazy">
            </span>
            <span class="pdp__ftilename">{{ name }}</span>
            <span class="pdp__ftilehm">
              HM {{ hm }}
              <span class="pdp__ftileprice">+{{ fee_label }}</span>
            </span>
            <span class="pdp__ftilemark" aria-hidden="true">{%- render 'icon', name: 'check' -%}</span>
          </button>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        Tapping a tile SELECTS; Confirm applies. Ed, 18/09/2026 — with 48
        near-identical photographs the shopper needs to put two side by side
        and change their mind, and an apply-on-tap grid closes under them the
        moment they touch the wrong one. Closing without confirming keeps the
        previous choice.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="pdp__modalfoot pdp__finishfoot">
        <span class="pdp__finishdraft">
          <img class="pdp__finishthumb pdp__finishthumb--sm"
               src="{{ polished_small }}"
               alt=""
               width="44"
               height="44"
               loading="lazy"
               data-fye-finish-draftimg>
          <span class="pdp__finishtxt">
            <span class="pdp__finishname" data-fye-finish-draftname>Polished</span>
            <span class="pdp__finishmeta" data-fye-finish-draftmeta>The standard finish, no extra work</span>
          </span>
        </span>
        {%- if pdx -%}
          <span class="pdx__pending" data-pdx-finish-total aria-live="polite"></span>
        {%- endif -%}
        <span class="pdp__finishbtns">
          <button type="button" class="btn btn--outline" data-fye-finish-cancel>Cancel</button>
          <button type="button" class="btn" data-fye-finish-confirm>{{ s.finish_confirm_label | default: 'Confirm finish' }}</button>
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

{%- comment -%}
  Loaded here rather than from the layout, so the ~3KB is requested only on
  the pages that render a picker. `defer` because nothing above depends on it
  and the markup is already usable without it.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script src="{{ 'fye-finish.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
